    "learn": [
      "Law versus instinct: The Jungle Law is a civilisation — study how Kipling makes rules feel as real as gravity.",
      "Colonial context: Read with awareness of British India; the stories encode empire even as they celebrate the wild.",
      "Coming of age: Mowgli's exit from the jungle mirrors every adolescent leaving a protected home.",
      "Animal character: Each species has distinct speech and ethics — a masterclass in voice.",
      "Global folklore: Kipling drew on Indian fables and his own invention to create myths that now feel ancient."
    ],
    "authorBio": "Rudyard Kipling (1865–1936) was born in British India, educated in England, and returned to journalism in Lahore and Allahabad before settling in England and later Sussex. He wrote Kim, Plain Tales from the Hills, the Just So Stories and patriotic verse that made him both celebrated and controversial. The Nobel committee praised his \"virile\" narrative gift. Kipling's politics were imperialist by modern standards, yet his craft — compression, dialect, suspense — remains studied worldwide. He died in 1936 and is buried in Poets' Corner, Westminster Abbey.",
    "whyRead": "Because it invented a modern mythography of the jungle that still shapes film, scouting culture and children's imaginations. Parents and teachers can discuss loyalty and law with young readers; adults can wrestle with its politics and artistry. The prose rewards reading aloud — Kipling meant these tales to be heard.",
    "historical": "Published when the British Raj was at its height, The Jungle Book reflects Victorian fascination with India as both exotic setting and imperial possession. American editions soon followed, and Mowgli became an international icon. Post-colonial criticism has re-examined Kipling's portrayals; the stories remain central to any honest conversation about literature and empire.",
